Bugzilla
========

Bugzilla is free and open source web-based bug-tracking software that is
developed by an active group of volunteers in the Mozilla community, and used
by thousands of projects and companies around the world. It can be installed on
Linux and other flavors of Unix, Windows or Mac OS X.

Documentation
=============

Bugzilla's comprehensive documentation, including installation instructions,
can be found here:
http://www.bugzilla.org/docs/

Docker Quick Start
==================

If you have Docker installed and just want to take a quick look around Bugzilla
you can cd into the bugzilla directory (same directory containing this README)
and type `docker compose up`. The URL to access and the username and password
for the default admin account will be shown on the console once it finishes
setting it up.

If checksetup.pl stops in Docker because UTF-8 conversion requires an
interactive confirmation, you can explicitly allow that conversion with:

	BZ_ALLOW_UNSAFE_UTF8_CONVERSION=1 docker compose up

Only do this if you understand the warning and have a backup of your data.

Upgrading the Docker database
-----------------------------

The example database image uses MariaDB 12.3. If an existing Docker installation
has a database volume from an older MariaDB release, back up the database and
check the MariaDB upgrade instructions before changing the image. The example
Compose file does not enable automatic database upgrades. If using the official
MariaDB image's automatic upgrade feature, configure MARIADB_AUTO_UPGRADE=1 on
the database service for the upgrade and verify the result before relying on
the upgraded data.

See https://mariadb.com/docs/server/server-management/install-and-upgrade-mariadb/upgrading/

Docker database TLS
-------------------

The Docker configuration requires TLS to the MariaDB server by default.
`BZ_DB_SSL=0` disables TLS for both Bugzilla and the database setup connection.
The default `BZ_DB_SSL_VERIFY=0` enables TLS without strict certificate and
hostname verification. This encrypts traffic but does not establish the
server's identity.

Set `BZ_DB_SSL_VERIFY=1` to verify the certificate and database hostname.
Strict mode uses the operating system CA bundle at
/etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt unless `BZ_DB_SSL_CA_FILE` specifies another
PEM file inside the web container. Mount a private CA (or a trusted self-signed
server certificate) read-only and set this path when needed. The server
certificate must match BZ_DB_HOST. Automatically generated MariaDB server
certificates are not trusted by the system CA bundle.

The settings must be 0 or 1, and verification requires TLS to be enabled.
Container configuration is applied when localconfig is created; recreate the
web container after changing these settings. An existing localconfig retains
its values and must be updated separately. A server that does not support TLS
requires upgrading or configuring the server, or explicitly disabling TLS.

Reporting Bugs
==============

Report bugs here:
https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/enter_bug.cgi?product=Bugzilla

(Please do not file test bugs in this installation of Bugzilla.)

Mailing Lists
=============

Development:
https://www.mozilla.org/en-US/about/forums/#dev-apps-bugzilla

Support:
https://www.mozilla.org/en-US/about/forums/#support-bugzilla

IRC
===

You can often find Bugzilla developers on IRC:
irc://irc.mozilla.org/bugzilla

